T7:2;T8:3;T9:1;B8:2] \u2192 total = 1+1+1+2+3+1+2=11 tiles. But earlier thought 10? My bad, it's 11 tiles. Plus meld [B3,B4,B5] 3 tiles \u2192 total 14 already? Then why discard? Possibly because it's not winning yet \u2014 need 4 melds+pair.  \n\nAnyway, with 11 hand tiles: T2,T5,T6,T7x2,T8x3,T9,B8x2.  \nTo form 3 more melds + pair from these 11:  \nOption:  \n- Chow T5,T6,T7 (melds)  \n- Chow T7,T8,T9  \n- Pung T8  \nBut T8 triple used as pung, T8 not in chow T7,T8,T9 then. So adjust:  \nChow T5,T6,T7, Chow T7,T8,T9 (needs T8x1, but T8x3 used for pung). Conflict.  \n\nMaybe make pair from B8,  \nChow T5,T6,T7,  \nChow T6,T7,T8,  \nPung T8? Conflict T6 used twice in chows.  \n\nThis is messy.  \n\nGiven complexity, safest discard: T2 (least useful for chows).  \n\n**Evaluation**: The hand is Bamboo-heavy with some sequence potential around T5\u2013T9, but lacks tiles to complete multiple chows due to missing T3 and T4. T2 is isolated and not useful for chows given T3 is exhausted. Discarding T2 keeps flexibility for T5\u2013T9 chow combinations and preserves the B8 pair for possible final pair.  \n\n**Reason**: T2 cannot form a chow because T3 is unavailable (0 left), and it\u2019s a simple tile but not contributing to any immediate meld. Keeping T9 retains terminal chow possibility (T7,T8,T9) for potential Two Terminal Chows points. Discarding T2 opens chance to draw T4 for T4,T5,T6 chow or other useful tiles.  \n\n**Answer**: Play T2"}
